## Borrowing: Access Without Ownership

Moving ownership is expensive — what if you just need to **look** at a value without taking it? That's where **borrowing** comes in.

### Shared Borrows (`&T`)

A shared borrow gives **read-only** access. Multiple shared borrows can exist at the same time:

```xiom
fn print_vec(v: &Vec[Int]) -> Unit {
  io.println("Length: " + v.len());  // can read, cannot modify
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let data = Vec[Int]();
  data.push(1);
  data.push(2);
  print_vec(&data);   // borrow, don't move
  print_vec(&data);   // can borrow again!
  return 0;
}
```

### Mutable Borrows (`&mut T`)

A mutable borrow gives **read-write** access. Only **one** mutable borrow can exist at a time, and no shared borrows while a mutable borrow is active:

```xiom
fn add_one(v: &mut Vec[Int]) -> Unit {
  v.push(v.len());  // can read and write
}

fn main() -> Int {
  var data = Vec[Int]();
  add_one(&mut data);  // mutable borrow
  let len = data.len(); // borrow ended, normal access restored
  return len;
}
```

### Borrow Rules (Enforced at Compile Time)

1. **Shared borrows** (`&T`): many at the same time, read-only
2. **Mutable borrows** (`&mut T`): exactly one at a time, read-write
3. **Cannot mix**: no shared borrows while a mutable borrow exists
4. **Borrows end**: when the borrowed reference goes out of scope

### Struct Parameters — Always Borrow

When a function takes a struct, borrow it instead of moving:

```xiom
pub type Point = { x: Float64; y: Float64; }

fn length(p: &Point) -> Float64 {
  return math.sqrt(p.x * p.x + p.y * p.y);
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let pt = Point{ x: 3.0, y: 4.0 };
  let l = length(&pt);  // borrow — pt still usable
  return l as Int;
}
```

### Why Borrowing Matters

Borrowing eliminates entire classes of bugs at compile time: no use-after-free, no double-free, no data races, and no dangling pointers — all without a garbage collector.

